\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine statistical regularities or training artifacts, evaluating four axes: classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and end-to-end learning (\S\ref{sec:gen:e2e}).

\subsection{Baseline Comparison}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares classification methods on the Pythia-1.4B nucleus setting.
Three feature-based methods---Random Forest (RF), CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020}, and DivEye \cite{diveye2026}---all operate on the same 19 statistical features and achieve statistically indistinguishable accuracy (78.1--78.2\%; pairwise bootstrap $p>0.05$ after Bonferroni correction, 95\% CI within $\pm$1.1pp).
These three significantly outperform CE-MLP ($p<0.001$, $+$3.8pp), which in turn trails logistic regression by $\sim$0.1pp.
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%), confirming that depth estimation requires the multi-dimensional feature representation defined in \S\ref{sec:method}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Comparison of classification methods on Pythia-1.4B nucleus. RF, CORAL, and DivEye use the same 19 statistical features and are statistically indistinguishable (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected). Chance = 25\%. 5-fold CV std: 0.2--0.9pp across all methods.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Method & 4-class Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
RF (19 features) & 78.2 \\
CORAL & 78.1 \\
DivEye & 78.2 \\
CE-MLP & 74.9 \\
LR & 75.0 \\
DeepSeek V3.2 (zero-shot)\footnotemark & 36.2 \\
ppl-regression (single feature) & 25.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\footnotetext{Evaluated on 500 samples (125 per class) from the Pythia-1.4B nucleus cell only, due to API cost; 95\% CI: $[32.0\%, 40.4\%]$. All other methods use the full 20{,}000-sample set.}
\end{table}

Identical performance across three structurally different classifiers confirms the signal resides in the features, not the architecture; a zero-shot LLM (DeepSeek V3.2) reaches only 36.2\%.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread across d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.4\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%).}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline while d2 drops to 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).
Excluding d0, d1--d3 balanced accuracy averages 55.4\% (chance = 33\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

We train on two decoding strategies and test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89.5\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%); excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy drops to 45.9\% (chance = 33\%), with d2 recall ranging from 3.7\% to 19\%---strategy-induced shifts pose a greater challenge than architectural differences.

\paragraph{Temperature-induced boundary inversion.}
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature inverts the d1/d2 boundary (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5; \S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).
Per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers the boundary but leaves a residual gap to within-strategy performance (per-model details in Limitations).

\subsection{End-to-End Learning}
\label{sec:gen:e2e}

DeBERTa-v3-base \cite{he2021_deberta_v3} and RoBERTa-base \cite{liu2019_roberta} finetuned end-to-end both converge to ${\sim}$59\% (19pp below RF 78.2\%; 1.2pp inter-backbone gap rules out architecture bias).
SHAP TreeExplainer shows perplexity and surprisal features account for 88.8\% of RF's contribution; three importance measures achieve strong concordance (Kendall's $W{=}0.90$, $p{<}0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}), while linear probing from DeBERTa hidden states yields negative $R^2$ for \textsc{var\_surprisal} and \textsc{p90\_ppl} (Appendix~\ref{app:feature_overlap}).
DCOL \cite{cloc2025} reached 64.5\% (Appendix~\ref{app:dcol_iterations}); all end-to-end approaches (58--65\%) underperform feature-based methods, suggesting depth estimation benefits from explicit tail statistics absent from PLM representations.

\section{Additional Results}
\label{app:additional}

\subsection{Top-$k$ Feature Trajectories}
\label{app:topk_trajectories}

Figure~\ref{fig:feature_trajectory} in the main text shows feature trajectories for nucleus ($p{=}0.95$) and temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$) sampling.
Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) trajectories follow the same monotonically decreasing trend for all features but with compressed inter-depth spacing, consistent with the signal attenuation effect discussed in \S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}.
Concretely, the d0$\to$d1 drop in \textsc{p90\_ppl} under top-$k$ is 78\% (vs.\ 87\% for nucleus), and the d2$\to$d3 reduction is 31\% (vs.\ 39\% for nucleus).
Despite the magnitude reduction, feature rank ordering across depths is preserved, which explains why ordinal classifiers maintain directionality even under top-$k$ conditions.

\subsection{Permutation Importance}
\label{app:permutation}

To verify that the Gini-based importance rankings in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features} are not an artifact of the impurity measure, we compute permutation importance (5-fold CV, 10 repeats) for all 9 atlas cells.
The top-2 features are stable across measures: \textsc{p90\_ppl} ranks in the top 2 in 9/9 cells under both Gini and permutation importance, and \textsc{var\_surprisal} in 8/9 (vs.\ 7/9 for Gini, with the additional cell being OLMo top-$k$; the single SHAP exception is GPT-2~XL top-$k$, where \textsc{p75\_ppl} displaces \textsc{var\_surprisal} to rank 3).
Kendall's $W{=}0.90$ across all three methods (Gini, permutation, SHAP) indicates strong concordance ($p{<}0.005$).
Pairwise Spearman correlations are: SHAP--Gini $\rho{=}0.93$, SHAP--permutation $\rho{=}0.84$, Gini--permutation $\rho{=}0.76$.
Per-cell top-5 overlap between SHAP and permutation importance is 3--4 out of 5 features; the aggregated (mean-across-cells) top-5 sets are identical, confirming that the top features are robust to the choice of importance measure at the aggregate level while per-cell rankings exhibit model-dependent variation.

\subsection{DCOL Iteration Details}
\label{app:dcol_iterations}

Table~\ref{tab:dcol_iterations} summarizes all six DCOL iterations.
DeBERTa-v3-large exhibited systematic numerical instability across five configurations; RoBERTa-large resolved stability but could not close the accuracy gap with feature-based methods.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{DCOL iteration summary. All experiments use Pythia-1.4B nucleus data. RF baseline: 78.2\%.}
\label{tab:dcol_iterations}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Ver. & Backbone & Config & Outcome \\
\midrule
v1 & DeBERTa-large & Full FT, margin\_lr$\,{=}\,10^{-3}$ & Embed.\ collapse (acc 31.5\%) \\
v2 & DeBERTa-large & Full FT, margin\_lr$\,{=}\,10^{-4}$ & OOM (GPU contention) \\
v3 & DeBERTa-large & Full FT, margin\_lr$\,{=}\,10^{-4}$ & Forgetting $+$ OOM \\
v4 & DeBERTa-large & LoRA $r{=}16$, m\_lr$\,{=}\,10^{-3}$ & Embed.\ collapse (v1 repeat) \\
v5 & DeBERTa-large & LoRA $r{=}16$, m\_lr$\,{=}\,10^{-4}$ & NaN gradient explosion \\
v6 & RoBERTa-large & Full FT, grad clip 1.0 & Stable; acc 64.5\% ($-$13.7pp) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Feature Overlap Analysis}
\label{app:feature_overlap}

We probe whether end-to-end PLM representations capture the same depth signals as our statistical features.
DeBERTa attention importance and RF permutation importance exhibit a significant negative correlation (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.484$, $p{=}0.036$; top-5 Jaccard overlap$\,{=}\,0$).
PLM representations excel at surface lexical features (TTR: $R^2{=}0.85$, repeated bigrams) but fail to capture perplexity distribution statistics---\textsc{var\_surprisal} and \textsc{p90\_ppl} yield negative $R^2$ under linear probing.
This confirms that the 19pp accuracy gap between feature-based and end-to-end methods (\S\ref{sec:gen:e2e}) stems from complementary---not redundant---representations: PLMs capture lexical patterns while the statistical features capture distributional tail properties that constitute the dominant depth markers.

\subsection{Domain Bias Analysis}
\label{app:domain_bias}

To assess whether the classifier exploits depth-related features or memorizes stylistic cues from individual seed articles, we conduct a leave-one-group-out (LOGO) evaluation where each fold holds out all passages derived from a single Wikipedia seed article.
Across all 9 atlas cells, 5-fold CV accuracy ranges from 59\% to 82\% (3.4$\times$ chance), confirming that depth signal is the dominant classification factor.
However, a residual domain signal exists: conditional on seed group, accuracy reaches 28--33\% (${\sim}$2$\times$ chance for the 4-class task), indicating that stylistic differences across seed articles contribute a secondary signal.
LOGO accuracy drops 8pp on average relative to random-split CV, with substantial variance across cells.
In the most affected configuration (Pythia-1.4B, top-$k$), individual LOGO folds drop as low as 22.9\%---approaching the 33.3\% chance level for d1--d3 classification---suggesting that in specific model--strategy combinations, the seed-article effect can nearly eliminate the depth signal for a held-out article group.
These results motivate future work on domain-robust features and cross-corpus evaluation.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Definition}
\label{sec:method:problem}

Let $f_\theta$ denote a language model and $\mathcal{D}_0$ a corpus of human-written seed text.
We define a \emph{recursive generation chain} of depth $K$ as the sequence $\mathcal{D}_0, \mathcal{D}_1, \dots, \mathcal{D}_K$, where each $\mathcal{D}_k$ ($k \geq 1$) is produced by finetuning $f_\theta$ on $\mathcal{D}_{k-1}$ and sampling from the resulting model.
Depth $d{=}0$ denotes human-written text; depth $d{=}k$ ($k \geq 1$) denotes text generated by a model trained on depth-$(k{-}1)$ data.

Given a text passage $x$, the task is to estimate its recursive generation depth $d \in \{0, 1, \dots, K\}$.
This is an ordinal classification problem: depths carry a natural ordering, and $d{=}1$ is structurally closer to $d{=}0$ than $d{=}3$ is, since each generation step compounds distributional drift.

\subsection{Multi-Generational Benchmark Construction}
\label{sec:method:benchmark}

We construct a benchmark spanning three axes: model family, decoding strategy, and generation depth.

\paragraph{Model families.}
We select three 1--1.5B-parameter models with diverse pretraining corpora and architectures: Pythia-1.4B (EleutherAI), OLMo-1B (AI2), and GPT-2~XL (OpenAI, 1.5B).
Restricting to a narrow parameter range isolates the effect of architecture and training data from scale.

\paragraph{Decoding strategies.}
Each model is paired with three decoding strategies: nucleus sampling ($p{=}0.95$) \cite{holtzman2020_nucleus}, temperature sampling ($\tau{=}0.9$), and top-$k$ sampling ($k{=}50$).
These span the dominant modes of open-ended generation and induce qualitatively different entropy profiles.
Our findings on feature importance and discriminability are limited to this grid; transfer to untested models, scales, or strategies is not established.

\paragraph{Depth and scale.}
The primary benchmark covers depths d0--d3 across all $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells, with 5{,}000 samples per depth per cell (180K samples total).
For depth extension experiments (\S\ref{sec:atlas}), we extend one pipeline (Pythia-1.4B, nucleus) to d0--d5.

\paragraph{Generation pipeline.}
Starting from a Wikipedia subset as seed text ($\mathcal{D}_0$), each generation cycle proceeds in three steps: (1)~LoRA finetuning \cite{hu2022lora} with rank $r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$, learning rate $2{\times}10^{-4}$, batch size 8, for 3 epochs; (2)~generation of 5{,}000 passages (max 512 tokens) under the specified decoding strategy; (3)~the generated corpus becomes $\mathcal{D}_k$ and serves as training data for the next cycle.
Each generation cycle samples unconditionally from the BOS token; there is no per-passage lineage across depths, ensuring that cross-validation splits do not introduce information leakage.
LoRA targets are architecture-specific: \texttt{query\_key\_value} for Pythia (GPT-NeoX), \texttt{q\_proj}/\texttt{k\_proj}/\texttt{v\_proj} for OLMo, and \texttt{c\_attn}/\texttt{c\_proj} for GPT-2~XL.
We adopt commonly used LoRA hyperparameters ($r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$, dropout$\,{=}\,0.05$) following \citet{hu2022lora}, applied uniformly across all models.

\paragraph{Reference model.}
All statistical features (\S\ref{sec:method:features}) are computed using a fixed reference model---GPT-2 base (124M)---that is independent of the three generation models.
The reference model computes per-token perplexity for each passage, providing a consistent measurement frame across model families.
In cross-model leave-one-out evaluation (\S\ref{sec:generalization}), the reference model remains fixed even when GPT-2~XL is held out; the partial architectural overlap this introduces is discussed in Limitations.

\subsection{Statistical Feature Design}
\label{sec:method:features}

We extract a 19-dimensional feature vector $\mathbf{f}(x)$ from the reference-model perplexity distribution over each passage $x$.
The features fall into four categories (Table~\ref{tab:features}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{The 19 statistical features comprising $\mathbf{f}(x)$, grouped by category. All perplexity and surprisal features are computed from the reference model (GPT-2 base, 124M).}
\label{tab:features}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llr@{}}
\toprule
Category & Features & $n$ \\
\midrule
Perplexity & mean, var, skewness, kurtosis, & 9 \\
statistics & p10, p25, p50, p75, p90 & \\
\addlinespace
Surprisal & mean, var, entropy & 3 \\
statistics & & \\
\addlinespace
Lexical & type--token ratio, hapax ratio, & 4 \\
diversity & bigram entropy, trigram entropy & \\
\addlinespace
Repetition & repeated 2/3/4-gram fractions & 3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\emph{Perplexity statistics} (9 features) capture the location, spread, and shape of the per-token perplexity distribution via its mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis, and five percentile quantiles (p10--p90).
The tail quantiles (p75, p90) prove especially informative: recursive finetuning erodes the high-perplexity tail, making these features monotonically decreasing with depth (\S\ref{sec:atlas}).

\emph{Surprisal statistics} (3 features) operate on $-\log p(\text{token})$ and include mean surprisal, surprisal variance, and the entropy of the surprisal distribution.
While correlated with perplexity features, surprisal variance captures second-order structure---the variability of token predictability---that is not redundant with perplexity moments.

\emph{Lexical diversity} (4 features) measures vocabulary usage through type--token ratio, hapax ratio (fraction of tokens appearing exactly once), and bigram/trigram entropy.

\emph{Repetition} (3 features) quantifies the fraction of repeated $n$-grams for $n \in \{2, 3, 4\}$, capturing the increased self-repetition characteristic of recursive generation.

\subsection{Effective Discriminable Depth}
\label{sec:method:edd}

Aggregate classification accuracy over $K{+}1$ classes obscures whether discriminability degrades at specific depths.
We introduce the \emph{Effective Discriminable Depth} (EDD), a scalar metric that summarizes how far into a recursive chain adjacent depths remain distinguishable.

For each adjacent depth pair $(d, d{+}1)$, we train a dedicated binary Random Forest (500 trees; increased from 200 in the multi-class setting to ensure convergence on the smaller per-pair samples) and compute the one-vs-one AUC.
Given a threshold $T$, we define:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{EDD}_T = \max \bigl\{ d^*{+}1 : \forall\, d' \leq d^*,\; \mathrm{AUC}(d', d'{+}1) \geq T \bigr\}
\label{eq:edd}
\end{equation}
where $d^* \in \{0, 1, \dots, K{-}1\}$.\footnote{Our AUC profiles are monotonically decreasing, so the $\forall$ and pointwise $\max\{d^*{+}1 : \mathrm{AUC}(d^*,d^*{+}1) \geq T\}$ formulations coincide; the $\forall$ version is preferred as it guarantees a contiguous discriminable range (see Limitations).}
The default threshold is $T{=}0.75$; we report sensitivity analyses at $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$ in \S\ref{sec:atlas}.
Crucially, each AUC is obtained from an independent binary pairwise classifier (5-fold stratified CV) rather than extracted from a single multi-class model, avoiding decision boundary dilution (\S\ref{sec:atlas}).

EDD captures a natural question: \emph{how deep into the recursive chain can we reliably distinguish successive generations?}
A high EDD indicates that the statistical fingerprints retain discriminative power across many recursion steps; a low EDD signals rapid convergence to an indistinguishable regime.

\paragraph{Classifiers.}
Our primary classifier is a Random Forest (200 trees, unconstrained depth, 5-fold stratified cross-validation).
We compare against CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020}, DivEye \cite{diveye2026}, a 2-layer cross-entropy MLP, logistic regression, and a single-feature baseline using mean perplexity alone; results appear in \S\ref{sec:generalization}.

